## (b) Solution for $Q=0$, $f=0$, $g=0$ (pure boundary driving)

With zero initial data and zero source, the only forcing is the inhomogeneous boundary condition $u(0,t)=h(t)$. Use d'Alembert's general solution

$$u(x,t)=F(x-ct)+G(x+ct).$$

**Initial conditions** (valid for arguments $>0$, i.e. $x>0$ at $t=0$):

$$u(x,0)=F(x)+G(x)=0,\qquad u_t(x,0)=-cF'(x)+cG'(x)=0.$$

From the first, $G=-F$; substituting into the second gives $-cF'-cF'=0\Rightarrow F'=0$, so $F=G=0$ for **positive** arguments. Hence $F(s)=G(s)=0$ for $s>0$.

**Boundary condition** at $x=0$:

$$u(0,t)=F(-ct)+G(ct)=h(t).$$

Since $ct>0$, $G(ct)=0$, leaving $F(-ct)=h(t)$, i.e.

$$F(s)=h\!\Big(-\frac{s}{c}\Big)\quad\text{for }s<0.$$

Now evaluate $u(x,t)=F(x-ct)+G(x+ct)$ at a point $(x,t)$ with $x>0$. Since $x+ct>0$, $G(x+ct)=0$. For $F(x-ct)$:

- If $x-ct>0$ (i.e. $t<x/c$): $F(x-ct)=0$.
- If $x-ct<0$ (i.e. $t>x/c$): $F(x-ct)=h\!\big(t-\tfrac{x}{c}\big)$.

Therefore

$$\boxed{\;u(x,t)=h\!\Big(t-\frac{x}{c}\Big)\,H\!\Big(t-\frac{x}{c}\Big),\qquad x>0,\ t\ge 0.\;}$$

The solution is a pure right-traveling wave: the boundary signal $h$ is simply translated to the right at speed $c$, delayed by the travel time $x/c$. (Consistency check: at $x=0$, $u(0,t)=h(t)\,H(t)=h(t)$ for $t\ge0$; at $t=0$, $u(x,0)=h(-x/c)H(-x/c)=0$ for $x>0$; and $u_t(x,0)=0$ for $x>0$. All conditions satisfied.)

---

## (c) When does $h(t)$ influence $u(x_1,t_1)$?

From part (b),

$$u(x_1,t_1)=h\!\Big(t_1-\frac{x_1}{c}\Big)\,H\!\Big(t_1-\frac{x_1}{c}\Big).$$

So $h$ influences $u(x_1,t_1)$ **iff** the argument $t_1-x_1/c\ge 0$, i.e.

$$\boxed{\;t_1\ge \frac{x_1}{c}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad t\ge \frac{x_1}{c}\ \text{is the earliest time the boundary affects }x_1.\;}$$

Equivalently, the boundary value $h(t)$ at a specific instant $t$ affects $u(x_1,t_1)$ precisely when $t=t_1-x_1/c\ge 0$.

**Physical interpretation.** Disturbances imposed at the boundary $x=0$ propagate into the domain at the finite wave speed $c$. A signal launched from the origin at time $t$ reaches the observation point $x_1$ at time $t+x_1/c$. Thus $u(x_1,t_1)$ can only "know about" boundary values $h(t)$ with $t\le t_1-x_1/c$; before the arrival time $t=x_1/c$ the point $x_1$ is still quiescent (the domain of dependence of $(x_1,t_1)$ does not yet intersect the boundary $x=0$). This is the half-line manifestation of finite propagation speed / the causal structure of the wave equation.

